--TEST--
`Core\Serialize::encode` takes no `inout` argument, and the value of an `inout` parameter is encoded as a copy — `rule:security/isolate-values-cross-by-copy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An `inout` argument is an alias to the caller's variable. Bytes outlive the
// call, so the alias cannot be written into them. Reading the parameter gives
// its value, and the value is encoded.
class Job {
    public static function save(inout array<int> $a): void {
        bytes $b = Core\Serialize::encode($a);
        echo Core\Bytes::length($b) > 0 ? "encoded" : "empty", "\n";
    }
}

// The accepted side: the value of `$a` is encoded.
array<int> $list = [1, 2];
Job::save(inout $list);

// Passing the variable itself as `inout` does not compile.
int $n = 1;
bytes $refused = Core\Serialize::encode(inout $n);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0714]: `inout` here names a by-value parameter
   --> case.nvs:18:41
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
